509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (The comparison results clearly demonstrate that both algorithms are capable of producing the same optimal path cost, confirming the correctness of both approaches. However, the Naive Search Algorithm suffers from high computational costs because it explores all possible paths without any optimization.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (Our empirical data validates this theoretical behavior; as the graph size increases, the Naive Search execution time spikes to 0.2450 ms in large-scale graphs, which is consistent with its theoretical complexity of O\(V!\). In contrast, the A* Search Algorithm improves performance by using a heuristic function to prioritize promising paths.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (The empirical results confirm this efficiency, as A*maintained a remarkably low execution time of 0.0238 ms even in complex environments, aligning perfectly with its theoretical complexity of O\(\(V + E\)\)log V\). As a result, A* demonstrates significantly better scalability, making it the only suitable solution for real-time crowd management systems in the Holy Mosque.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
