516 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (This project presented a comparative study between the Naive Search Algorithm and the A* Search Algorithm for solving the shortest path problem within a graph-based model representing crowd movement in the Holy Mosque environment.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The experimental results demonstrated that both algorithms are capable of producing the same optimal path, confirming the correctness and reliability of both approaches. However, clear differences were observed in terms of computational efficiency, scalability, and overall performance.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The Naive Search Algorithm relies on exhaustive exploration of all possible paths, which leads to high computational cost and increased execution time as graph complexity grows. Consequently, its practicality decreases significantly in large-scale or real-time environments.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
519 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (In contrast, the A* Search Algorithm achieves superior performance by utilizing a heuristic function to intelligently guide the search process toward the target node. This approach minimizes unnecessary exploration, reduces execution time, and maintains optimal results even as the graph size increases.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
520 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (f\(n\)=g\(n\)+h\(n\))
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
521 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Based on both theoretical and empirical analysis, the A* Search Algorithm proved to be the more efficient and scalable solution. Therefore, it represents a highly suitable approach for real-world smart crowd management systems, where fast, accurate, and reliable route optimization is essential for ensuring safe and efficient movement within highly crowded environments such as the Holy Mosque during Umrah.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
